    <!-- Key applied ethics concepts visual -->
    <div class="content-panel u-mb-lg">
      <div class="panel-title-spaced">Five essential concepts in contemporary ethics</div>
      <div class="card-grid-large">
        <div class="sub-card-roomy">
          <div class="small-title">The doctrine of double effect</div>
          <div class="muted-copy-tight u-mt-sm">Originating in Aquinas, developed by modern just war theorists: it is sometimes permissible to cause a harm as a foreseen but unintended side-effect of bringing about a good result, even if it would be impermissible to cause that harm intentionally. Four conditions: (1) the action itself is not intrinsically wrong; (2) the agent intends the good effect; (3) the bad effect is foreseen but not intended; (4) there is proportionate reason. Used in: medical ethics (giving pain-relieving doses that may hasten death), military ethics (civilian casualties from legitimate military operations).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-card-roomy">
          <div class="small-title">The separateness of persons</div>
          <div class="muted-copy-tight u-mt-sm">Rawls's objection to utilitarianism: utilitarianism, in maximising aggregate welfare, treats society as if it were a single person and can justify sacrificing some individuals for others. But persons are distinct -- my suffering cannot be compensated by your happiness. Rights and justice constrain the maximisation of aggregate welfare precisely because they respect the fact that each person's life is their own, not fungible with others'. This is one of the most important arguments in 20th-century political philosophy.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-card-roomy">
          <div class="small-title">Effective altruism</div>
          <div class="muted-copy-tight u-mt-sm">Peter Singer's argument (Famine, Affluence, and Morality, 1972): if we can prevent something bad from happening without sacrificing anything of comparable moral importance, we ought to do it. Distance and proximity are morally irrelevant. If you can save a drowning child at the cost of ruining your expensive shoes, you should -- but the same logic applies to donating money to prevent deaths from malaria. EA as a movement: use evidence and reason to identify the most cost-effective ways to do good. Critiques: agent-partiality may be legitimate; demanding impartiality may be self-undermining; systemic change matters more than individual donations.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-card-roomy">
          <div class="small-title">Moral status and marginal cases</div>
          <div class="muted-copy-tight u-mt-sm">What determines whether a being has moral status (deserves moral consideration)? Candidates: rationality (Kant -- but this excludes infants and severely cognitively impaired humans), sentience/capacity for suffering (Singer -- includes animals), being human (speciesism? -- Singer's argument: if the same criterion that excludes animals also excludes some humans, it cannot be the morally relevant property). The argument from marginal cases: if severely cognitively impaired humans have full moral status, then whatever property grounds this must also apply to many non-human animals.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-card-roomy">
          <div class="small-title">Future generations and long-termism</div>
          <div class="muted-copy-tight u-mt-sm">If there will be vastly more people in the future than there are now, the welfare of future people might swamp the welfare of present people in any calculation. Longtermism (William MacAskill, Toby Ord): reducing the risk of human extinction or permanent civilisational collapse is the most important moral priority -- even a small reduction in existential risk has enormous expected value. Objections: non-identity problem (Parfit) -- we cannot harm people by causing them to exist; the replaceability objection; demandingness. The philosophical foundations of AI safety concern are partly longtermist.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-card-roomy">
          <div class="small-title">Care ethics</div>
          <div class="muted-copy-tight u-mt-sm">Carol Gilligan (In a Different Voice, 1982) argued that Kohlberg's stages of moral development were biased toward a "justice orientation" (impartial rules, rights, universality) and against a "care orientation" (relationships, context, responsiveness to particular others). Nel Noddings developed care ethics into a full moral theory: the caring relationship, not universal principles, is the basic moral unit. Care ethics has been particularly influential in nursing ethics, feminist philosophy, and critiques of the welfare state as modelled on impersonal bureaucratic justice rather than particular care relationships.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

