      'Spinoza':      'Spinoza (1632–1677) — Rationalist pantheist. God = Nature (Deus sive Natura). All of reality is one infinite substance with infinite attributes. Humans are modes of this substance. Geometric method: proved philosophy like Euclid.',
      'Locke':        'Locke (1632–1704) — Father of liberalism. Mind as blank slate (tabula rasa). Natural rights: life, liberty, property. Government by consent; right of revolution. His Two Treatises directly inspired the American Declaration of Independence.',
      'Leibniz':      'Leibniz (1646–1716) — Rationalist polymath (co-invented calculus). Monads: the universe consists of non-spatial, non-interacting mind-like substances. Pre-established harmony. This is the best of all possible worlds. Founded formal logic.',
      'Hume':         'Hume (1711–1776) — The most radical empiricist. Induction is not rationally justified. Causation is habitual expectation, not necessity. The self is a bundle of perceptions. Is-ought gap. Undermined natural theology. "Woke Kant from dogmatic slumber."',
      'Rousseau':     'Rousseau (1712–1778) — The general will: legitimate authority derives from the collective will of the people, not rulers. Humans are naturally good; society corrupts. "Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains." Influenced the French Revolution.',
      'Kant':         'Kant (1724–1804) — The most important modern philosopher. Synthesised rationalism and empiricism. The categorical imperative. Phenomena vs noumena. Transcendental idealism. His critical philosophy transformed epistemology, ethics, and aesthetics.',
      'Bentham':      'Bentham (1748–1832) — Founded Utilitarianism: the greatest happiness of the greatest number. The felicific calculus: pleasures and pains can be measured and compared. Invented the panopticon prison. His embalmed body still sits at University College London.',
      'Hegel':        'Hegel (1770–1831) — The most ambitious systematic philosopher. The dialectic: thesis-antithesis-synthesis. History is the progressive self-realisation of Spirit (Geist) toward freedom. Influenced Marx, Kierkegaard, and almost all later philosophy.',
      'Schopenhauer': 'Schopenhauer (1788–1860) — The world as will and representation. Beneath all phenomena lies blind, purposeless Will. Life is suffering; salvation through aesthetic contemplation and asceticism. Influenced Nietzsche, Wagner, Freud, and Buddhism\'s Western reception.',
      'Mill':         'Mill (1806–1873) — Refined utilitarianism: pleasures differ in quality, not just quantity. "Better to be Socrates dissatisfied than a fool satisfied." On Liberty: the harm principle. The Subjection of Women: an early feminist classic.',
      'Kierkegaard':  'Kierkegaard (1813–1855) — Father of existentialism. Three stages of existence: aesthetic, ethical, religious. The leap of faith. Attacked the complacency of official Christianity. Wrote under pseudonyms to force readers to choose.',
      'Marx':         'Marx (1818–1883) — Dialectical materialism: history is driven by class conflict over the means of production. Alienation under capitalism. "Philosophers have interpreted the world; the point is to change it." With Engels: The Communist Manifesto.',
      'Nietzsche':    'Nietzsche (1844–1900) — God is dead. The will to power. The Ubermensch. Eternal recurrence. Master vs slave morality. Perspectivism: there are no facts, only interpretations. The most influential, most misread philosopher of the 19th century.',
      'Frege':        'Frege (1848–1925) — Father of analytic philosophy and modern logic. Developed first-order predicate logic. Discovered the morning star/evening star puzzle. Russell\'s paradox undermined his life\'s work days before publication.',
      'Husserl':      'Husserl (1859–1938) — Founded phenomenology: the rigorous description of conscious experience as it presents itself. Intentionality: all consciousness is consciousness of something. His method influenced Heidegger, Sartre, and Merleau-Ponty.',
      'Russell':      'Russell (1872–1970) — Co-wrote Principia Mathematica with Whitehead. Theory of descriptions. Logical atomism. Popular writer on philosophy, education, and pacifism. Nobel Prize for Literature. Active in CND marches into his 90s.',
      'Wittgenstein': 'Wittgenstein (1889–1951) — Two philosophies. Early: the world is all that is the case; language pictures facts; "Whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent." Late: meaning is use; language games; private language argument.',
      'Heidegger':    'Heidegger (1889–1976) — Being and Time: Dasein is always already "thrown" into a world. Authenticity vs das Man (the they). Being-toward-death. Later work on technology and the history of Being. Deeply compromised by Nazi membership.',
      'Sartre':       'Sartre (1905–1980) — Existence precedes essence. Radical freedom and responsibility. Bad faith. Being-in-itself vs being-for-itself. "Hell is other people." Refused the Nobel Prize. Combined existentialism with Marxism in his later work.',
      'Rawls':        'Rawls (1921–2002) — A Theory of Justice (1971): the most important work of political philosophy of the 20th century. The veil of ignorance and original position. The difference principle. Revived liberal political philosophy against utilitarianism.',
      'Singer':       'Peter Singer (b.1946) — The most controversial living philosopher. Utilitarian. Animal liberation: suffering, not species, is the morally relevant criterion. The drowning child argument for global poverty relief. Founder of effective altruism.',
    };

    var svg = document.getElementById('phil-timeline-svg');
    var tooltip = document.getElementById('phil-tl-tooltip');
    if (!svg) return;

    var W = 760, H = 310;
    var PAD_L = 6, PAD_R = 10, PAD_T = 28, ROW_H = 18, GAP = 3;
    var YEAR_MIN = -500, YEAR_MAX = 2030;
    var CHART_W = W - PAD_L - PAD_R;

    function xOf(year) {
      return PAD_L + (year - YEAR_MIN) / (YEAR_MAX - YEAR_MIN) * CHART_W;
    }

    // Era bands
    var eras = [
      { label:'Ancient', start:-500, end:0,    fill:'rgba(60,52,137,0.07)' },
      { label:'Late Antiquity', start:0, end:600,   fill:'rgba(113,43,19,0.07)' },
      { label:'Medieval / Islamic Golden Age', start:600, end:1400,  fill:'rgba(8,80,65,0.07)' },
      { label:'Early Modern', start:1400, end:1700, fill:'rgba(99,56,6,0.07)' },
      { label:'Modern', start:1700, end:1900,  fill:'rgba(12,68,124,0.07)' },
      { label:'Contemporary', start:1900, end:2030,  fill:'rgba(68,68,65,0.09)' },
    ];

    var isDark = (function() {
      var t = document.documentElement.getAttribute('data-theme');
      if (t === 'dark') return true;
      if (t === 'light') return false;
      return window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
    })();

    var axisCol = isDark ? 'rgba(255,255,255,0.18)' : 'rgba(0,0,0,0.12)';
    var labelCol = isDark ? 'rgba(255,255,255,0.35)' : 'rgba(0,0,0,0.35)';
    var eraLabelCol = isDark ? 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)' : 'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)';

    // Axis ticks
    var ticks = [-500, -400, -300, -200, -100, 0, 200, 400, 600, 800, 1000, 1200, 1400, 1600, 1800, 2000];

    var svgContent = '';

    // Era bands
    eras.forEach(function(e) {
      var x1 = xOf(e.start), x2 = xOf(e.end);
      svgContent += '<rect x="' + x1 + '" y="' + PAD_T + '" width="' + (x2-x1) + '" height="' + (5 * (ROW_H + GAP)) + '" fill="' + e.fill + '"/>';
      svgContent += '<text x="' + ((x1+x2)/2) + '" y="14" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5" fill="' + eraLabelCol + '" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif">' + e.label + '</text>';
    });

    // Axis line
    svgContent += '<line x1="' + PAD_L + '" y1="' + (PAD_T + 5*(ROW_H+GAP) + 4) + '" x2="' + (W - PAD_R) + '" y2="' + (PAD_T + 5*(ROW_H+GAP) + 4) + '" stroke="' + axisCol + '" stroke-width="0.5"/>';

    // Tick marks and labels
    ticks.forEach(function(yr) {
      var x = xOf(yr);
      svgContent += '<line x1="' + x + '" y1="' + (PAD_T + 5*(ROW_H+GAP) + 4) + '" x2="' + x + '" y2="' + (PAD_T + 5*(ROW_H+GAP) + 10) + '" stroke="' + axisCol + '" stroke-width="0.5"/>';
      var label = yr < 0 ? Math.abs(yr) + ' BCE' : (yr === 0 ? '0' : yr + ' CE');
      svgContent += '<text x="' + x + '" y="' + (PAD_T + 5*(ROW_H+GAP) + 20) + '" text-anchor="middle" font-size="7.5" fill="' + labelCol + '" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif">' + label + '</text>';
    });

    // Philosopher bars
    philosophers.forEach(function(p) {
      var x1 = xOf(p.birth);
      var x2 = xOf(Math.min(p.death, 2025));
      var bw = Math.max(x2 - x1, 4);
      var y = PAD_T + p.row * (ROW_H + GAP);
      var pid = 'bar-' + p.name.replace(/[^a-z]/gi,'');
      svgContent += '<rect id="' + pid + '" class="phil-tl-bar" x="' + x1 + '" y="' + y + '" width="' + bw + '" height="' + ROW_H + '" rx="3" fill="' + p.col + '" opacity="0.82" data-name="' + p.name + '"/>';
      // Label if bar wide enough
      if (bw > 38) {
        svgContent += '<text x="' + (x1 + 4) + '" y="' + (y + ROW_H - 5) + '" font-size="8" fill="rgba(255,255,255,0.95)" font-family="system-ui,sans-serif" pointer-events="none">' + p.name + '</text>';
      }
    });

    svg.innerHTML = svgContent;
    svg.setAttribute('viewBox', '0 0 ' + W + ' ' + (PAD_T + 5*(ROW_H+GAP) + 28));

    // Click interaction
    svg.addEventListener('click', function(e) {
      var bar = e.target.closest('.phil-tl-bar');
      if (!bar) return;
      var name = bar.getAttribute('data-name');
      // Highlight
      svg.querySelectorAll('.phil-tl-bar').forEach(function(b) { b.style.opacity = '0.45'; });
      bar.style.opacity = '1';
      tooltip.innerHTML = info[name] || name;
    });

    svg.addEventListener('mouseover', function(e) {
      var bar = e.target.closest('.phil-tl-bar');
      if (bar) bar.style.cursor = 'pointer';
    });
  })();


  /* ──────────────────────────────────────────
     ETHICAL THEORY SCENARIO QUIZ
  ────────────────────────────────────────── */
  (function() {
    // Each question has choices tagged D=deontology, V=virtue, C=consequentialist
    var questions = [
      {
        scenario: 'A runaway trolley will kill five people. You can divert it, killing one bystander. The one person happens to be a brilliant surgeon who will save hundreds of lives next year — the five are retired. What do you do?',
        choices: [
          { letter:'A', text:'Do not divert — it is wrong to use any person as a means to save others.', tag:'D' },
          { letter:'B', text:'Divert — five lives saved outweighs one, regardless of who they are.', tag:'C' },
          { letter:'C', text:'Reflect on what a person of practical wisdom would do — the calculus alone feels insufficient.', tag:'V' },
        ]
      },
      {
        scenario: 'A close friend confesses they have been committing minor tax fraud — not enough to harm anyone noticeably. They ask you to keep this secret. What do you do?',
        choices: [
          { letter:'A', text:'Respect the confidence — loyalty to friends is a genuine moral value, not just preference.', tag:'V' },
          { letter:'B', text:'Report it — honesty is an unconditional duty and law-breaking cannot be justified by friendship.', tag:'D' },
          { letter:'C', text:'Weigh the real-world harms: the fraud is trivial; reporting would cause serious harm to your friend for minimal social benefit.', tag:'C' },
        ]
      },
      {
        scenario: 'You are a doctor in a disaster. You have five dying patients who each need a different organ. A healthy visitor walks in. Could you kill them to harvest their organs and save the five?',
        choices: [
          { letter:'A', text:'No — killing an innocent person is absolutely prohibited, regardless of the arithmetic.', tag:'D' },
          { letter:'B', text:'Yes — five lives saved vs one life lost; the outcome is clearly better.', tag:'C' },
          { letter:'C', text:'No — a person of good character could never do this; the kind of person it would make you matters as much as the outcome.', tag:'V' },
        ]
      },
      {
        scenario: 'You could donate a significant portion of your income to an effective charity that would save multiple lives. You would have to give up expensive holidays and luxuries. Are you morally obligated to do this?',
        choices: [
          { letter:'A', text:'Yes — the aggregate welfare gain far exceeds the loss; failing to donate when you could is morally equivalent to letting people die.', tag:'C' },
          { letter:'B', text:'No — you have duties not to harm others, but no strict duty to benefit strangers at serious personal cost.', tag:'D' },
          { letter:'C', text:'Generosity is a virtue, but virtue requires balance — total self-sacrifice can undermine your own flourishing and relationships.', tag:'V' },
        ]
      },
      {
        scenario: 'A politician discovers that telling a small, harmless lie will prevent a serious injustice. Should they lie?',
        choices: [
          { letter:'A', text:'No — lying is intrinsically wrong regardless of consequences; honesty is a categorical duty.', tag:'D' },
          { letter:'B', text:'Yes — the outcome prevents injustice; consequences are what ultimately matter.', tag:'C' },
          { letter:'C', text:'It depends on the full context and character of the politician — a person of integrity sometimes faces tragic choices where no option is clean.', tag:'V' },
        ]
      },
    ];

    var scores = { D: 0, V: 0, C: 0 };
    var current = 0;
    var answered = [];

    var results = {
      D: {
        title: 'Deontologist',
        color: '#0C447C',
        body: 'Your instincts consistently prioritise rules, duties, and rights over outcomes. You are closest to Kant\'s deontological ethics: some actions are intrinsically right or wrong regardless of their consequences. You are drawn to the idea that persons must never be treated merely as means, and that obligations like honesty and non-harm hold even when breaking them would produce better outcomes. Key thinkers to explore: Kant (Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals), W.D. Ross (The Right and the Good), Christine Korsgaard (Creating the Kingdom of Ends).'
      },
      V: {
        title: 'Virtue ethicist',
        color: '#27500A',
        body: 'Your instincts resist both pure rule-following and pure calculation. You are closest to Aristotelian virtue ethics: what matters is not just what you do but who you are — the character, practical wisdom, and human relationships that constitute a good life. You are uncomfortable with both mechanical rule-application and cold utilitarian arithmetic. Key thinkers to explore: Aristotle (Nicomachean Ethics), Alasdair MacIntyre (After Virtue), Martha Nussbaum (The Fragility of Goodness), Philippa Foot (Natural Goodness).'
      },
      C: {
        title: 'Consequentialist',
        color: '#712B13',
        body: 'Your instincts consistently focus on outcomes, welfare, and the balance of benefits and harms across all affected parties. You are closest to consequentialism — the view that the right action is whatever produces the best overall outcome. You are suspicious of rules that ignore consequences and comfortable applying impartial reasoning even when it is uncomfortable. Key thinkers to explore: Jeremy Bentham (Introduction to Principles of Morals and Legislation), John Stuart Mill (Utilitarianism), Peter Singer (Practical Ethics), Derek Parfit (Reasons and Persons).'
      }
    };

    var progressEl = document.getElementById('quiz-progress');
    var bodyEl = document.getElementById('quiz-body');
    var resultEl = document.getElementById('quiz-result');
    var resultTitle = document.getElementById('quiz-result-title');
    var resultBody = document.getElementById('quiz-result-body');
    var restartBtn = document.getElementById('quiz-restart');
    if (!progressEl || !bodyEl) return;

    function renderProgress() {
      progressEl.innerHTML = '';
      questions.forEach(function(_, i) {
        var pip = document.createElement('div');
        pip.className = 'quiz-pip' + (i < current ? ' done' : (i === current ? ' active' : ''));
        progressEl.appendChild(pip);
      });
    }

    function renderQuestion() {
      var q = questions[current];
      bodyEl.innerHTML = '';
      var scenarioEl = document.createElement('div');
      scenarioEl.className = 'quiz-scenario';
      scenarioEl.textContent = q.scenario;
      bodyEl.appendChild(scenarioEl);
      var choicesEl = document.createElement('div');
      choicesEl.className = 'quiz-choices';
      q.choices.forEach(function(c) {
        var btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.className = 'quiz-choice';
        btn.innerHTML = '<span class="quiz-letter">' + c.letter + '</span><span>' + c.text + '</span>';
        btn.addEventListener('click', function() {
          if (answered[current] !== undefined) return;
          answered[current] = c.tag;
          scores[c.tag]++;
          // Highlight selection
          choicesEl.querySelectorAll('.quiz-choice').forEach(function(b) { b.classList.remove('selected'); });
          btn.classList.add('selected');
          // Advance after brief pause
          setTimeout(function() {
            current++;
            if (current >= questions.length) {
              showResult();
            } else {
              renderProgress();
              renderQuestion();
            }
          }, 380);
        });
        choicesEl.appendChild(btn);
      });
      bodyEl.appendChild(choicesEl);
    }

    function showResult() {
      bodyEl.style.display = 'none';
      progressEl.innerHTML = '';
      // Find winner
      var winner = 'V';
      if (scores.D >= scores.V && scores.D >= scores.C) winner = 'D';
      else if (scores.C >= scores.V && scores.C >= scores.D) winner = 'C';
      var r = results[winner];
      resultTitle.textContent = 'Your closest framework: ' + r.title;
      resultTitle.style.color = r.color;
      // Score breakdown
      var breakdown = document.createElement('div');
      breakdown.style.cssText = 'font-size:11px; color:var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom:8px;';
      breakdown.textContent = 'Score breakdown — Deontology: ' + scores.D + '/5 · Virtue ethics: ' + scores.V + '/5 · Consequentialism: ' + scores.C + '/5';
      resultBody.appendChild(breakdown);
      var bodyText = document.createElement('div');
      bodyText.style.cssText = 'font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.6;';
      bodyText.textContent = r.body;
      resultBody.appendChild(bodyText);
      resultEl.classList.add('show');
    }

    restartBtn.addEventListener('click', function() {
      scores = { D: 0, V: 0, C: 0 };
      current = 0;
      answered = [];
      resultEl.classList.remove('show');
      resultBody.innerHTML = '';
      bodyEl.style.display = '';
      renderProgress();
      renderQuestion();
    });

    renderProgress();
    renderQuestion();
  })();


  /* ──────────────────────────────────────────
     UTILITY: generic explorer builder
  ────────────────────────────────────────── */
  function buildExplorer(btnContainerId, displayId, dataObj, defaultKey) {
    const container = document.getElementById(btnContainerId);
    const display   = document.getElementById(displayId);
    let activeKey   = null;

    Object.keys(dataObj).forEach(function(key) {
      const btn = document.createElement('button');
      btn.className   = 'phil-btn';
      btn.textContent = dataObj[key].name;
      btn.addEventListener('click', function() {
        container.querySelectorAll('.phil-btn').forEach(function(b){ b.classList.remove('active'); });
        btn.classList.add('active');
        activeKey = key;
        renderDisplay(key);
      });
      container.appendChild(btn);
    });

    function renderDisplay(key) {
      const d = dataObj[key];
      display.innerHTML = d.render(d);
    }

    // activate default
    if (defaultKey && dataObj[defaultKey]) {
      const btns = container.querySelectorAll('.phil-btn');
      btns.forEach(function(b){
        if (b.textContent === dataObj[defaultKey].name) b.classList.add('active');
      });
      renderDisplay(defaultKey);
    }
  }

