  const ethicsExplains = [
    { min:0,  max:15, text:'<strong>Pure deontology</strong> — Kant\'s categorical imperative: act only according to rules you could universalise. Consequences are morally irrelevant. The wrongness of murder does not depend on outcomes; it violates a rational being\'s dignity unconditionally. Lying is always wrong, even to a murderer asking where your friend is hiding.' },
    { min:15, max:35, text:'<strong>Moderate deontology (W.D. Ross)</strong> — we have a set of prima facie duties (keep promises, tell truth, do not harm, repay debts). When they conflict, we use judgement. This is less rigid than Kant\'s absolute rules — sometimes consequences inform which duty takes priority — but the duties are not derived from outcomes.' },
    { min:35, max:65, text:'<strong>Virtue ethics (Aristotle)</strong> — right action is what a virtuous person of practical wisdom (phronesis) would do. There are no fixed rules; character and context determine the answer. The good life (eudaimonia) is flourishing — exercising distinctly human capacities (reason, relationship, civic life) with excellence. Ask not "what is the right rule?" or "what maximises welfare?" but "what kind of person should I be?"' },
    { min:65, max:85, text:'<strong>Moderate consequentialism (Mill)</strong> — pleasure and pain are what ultimately matter, but pleasures differ in quality as well as quantity. Mental and moral pleasures are "higher" than physical ones. Individual rights function as rules of thumb that generally maximise welfare, so respecting them is usually the right strategy — not a deontological constraint but a consequence of experience.' },
    { min:85, max:100, text:'<strong>Pure consequentialism (Bentham / Singer)</strong> — the right act is whatever maximises the sum of welfare across all affected sentient beings. Every person (and animal) counts for one, none for more than one. No action is intrinsically wrong — only instrumentally, if it produces worse outcomes. This logic, taken seriously, demands radical impartiality: you must give to effective charities until the marginal disutility of giving equals the marginal good you produce.' },
