      keyideas:'The Consolation of Philosophy, written while awaiting execution for treason, argues through dialogue with Lady Philosophy that true goods (virtue, wisdom, God) cannot be taken away by fortune, while apparent goods (wealth, power, fame) are contingent and empty. Fortune\'s wheel: earthly goods rise and fall by chance; wisdom consists in not depending on them. Providence and free will: God\'s eternal foreknowledge does not eliminate human freedom because God perceives all time simultaneously — eternity is the complete, simultaneous possession of unlimited life, not endless duration. On universals: introduced the problem that defined medieval philosophy — do general terms (humanity, redness) refer to real things or only to mental concepts?',
      legacy:'His translation of Aristotle\'s logic gave medieval scholars their only sustained contact with Greek philosophy for centuries. The Consolation influenced Alfred the Great, Chaucer, Dante, and Queen Elizabeth I (who translated it). His formulation of the problem of universals set the agenda for 800 years of medieval debate between realists and nominalists.',
