      keyideas:'The Floating Man thought experiment (anticipating Descartes by 600 years): imagine a man created in mid-air, blindfolded, unable to feel his own body. He would still be aware of his own existence as a thinking self. Therefore the soul\'s existence is self-evident and independent of the body. Necessary vs contingent being: God is the Necessary Being whose existence follows from His own nature; all other things are contingent, requiring a cause. This became the basis of Aquinas\'s Third Way. The distinction between essence and existence: in all contingent things, what a thing is (essence) is distinct from the fact that it exists. Only in God are they identical. Avicennian logic: extended Aristotle\'s syllogistic in ways that influenced both Islamic and European logic.',
      legacy:'His Latin translation (as Avicenna) shaped European medicine until the 17th century. His philosophical works, translated into Latin in the 12th century, were the primary stimulus for Scholasticism. Albert the Great and Thomas Aquinas engaged deeply with his arguments. His floating man argument is the first clear formulation of the cogito-style proof. He is the most important philosopher between Aristotle and Aquinas.',
