  var earlyModPhilosophers = {
    descartes: { name:'René Descartes', years:'1596–1650', origin:'La Haye en Touraine, France', col:'#3C3489', bg:'#EEEDFE',
      wrote:'Discourse on the Method (1637), Meditations on First Philosophy (1641), Principles of Philosophy (1644).',
      contribution:'The father of modern philosophy. Broke from Scholastic Aristotelianism entirely. Applied mathematical method to philosophy. His radical doubt and the Cogito established a new starting point for all subsequent epistemology. Developed mind-body dualism (Cartesian dualism) — the view that mind and body are fundamentally distinct substances.',
      keyideas:'Methodological doubt: doubt everything that can possibly be doubted to find what is indubitable. Cogito ergo sum: "I think therefore I am" — the indubitable foundation. Mind-body dualism: res cogitans (thinking substance) vs res extensa (extended substance). The problem this creates (how do mind and body interact?) has never been solved. Clear and distinct perception as the criterion of truth. God\'s existence as a guarantee of clear and distinct ideas.',
      legacy:'Cartesian dualism shaped Western understanding of mind and body for centuries — it\'s the default folk psychology even now. The mind-body problem it creates is still central to philosophy of mind. His method (start from certain foundations, build up) defined the rationalist tradition. He also invented analytical geometry (Cartesian coordinates) and made major advances in optics.',
