    hobbes: { name:'Thomas Hobbes', years:'1588–1679', origin:'Westport, Wiltshire, England', col:'#444441', bg:'#F1EFE8',
      wrote:'Leviathan (1651) — one of the most important and provocative works in political philosophy. De Cive (1642), De Corpore (1655).',
      contribution:'The first systematic materialist philosopher of the modern period. Applied mechanistic explanation (the new science of Galileo) to human nature and politics. His Leviathan founded modern political philosophy by deriving the necessity of the state from human nature and rational self-interest.',
      keyideas:'Materialism: everything that exists is matter in motion, including thought and sensation. The state of nature: without political authority, human life is "solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short" — a war of all against all. Social contract: individuals rationally agree to surrender natural freedom to a sovereign (the Leviathan) in exchange for security. The sovereign\'s power must be absolute — divided authority leads back to the state of nature. Subjects have no right to rebel except to defend their own lives.',
      legacy:'Hobbes\'s bleak account of human nature (rational self-interest, competitive) directly challenged both Christian optimism and classical virtue ethics. His social contract theory (though different in conclusions) set the framework for Locke and Rousseau. His materialism about mind anticipated eliminative materialism and neuroscience by centuries. Realists in international relations theory often cite Hobbes.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    spinoza: { name:'Baruch Spinoza', years:'1632–1677', origin:'Amsterdam, Dutch Republic', col:'#085041', bg:'#E1F5EE',
      wrote:'Ethics (published posthumously 1677 — written more geometrico, in Euclidean style), Tractatus Theologico-Politicus (1670), On the Improvement of the Understanding.',
      contribution:'The most systematic and rigorous rationalist. Excommunicated from the Amsterdam Jewish community at age 23 — the harshest such excommunication (cherem) on record. His pantheism (God = Nature) and biblical criticism were revolutionary. Widely regarded as one of the most profound metaphysicians in the Western tradition.',
      keyideas:'Substance monism: there is only one substance, which Spinoza calls both God and Nature (Deus sive Natura). Mind and body are two attributes of the same substance — not two substances (solving Descartes\'s interaction problem). Determinism: everything follows necessarily from God\'s nature; free will is an illusion arising from ignorance of causes. Human freedom consists in understanding necessity and acting from reason rather than passion. Sub specie aeternitatis: seeing things "under the aspect of eternity."',
      legacy:'Einstein said he believed in "Spinoza\'s God" — an impersonal God identical with the laws of nature. Hegel and the German Idealists were deeply influenced by his monism. He provided the first serious argument for democracy based on freedom of thought. His biblical criticism pioneered modern historical scholarship of religious texts.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    locke: { name:'John Locke', years:'1632–1704', origin:'Wrington, Somerset, England', col:'#0C447C', bg:'#E6F1FB',
      wrote:'An Essay Concerning Human Understanding (1689), Two Treatises of Government (1689), A Letter Concerning Toleration (1689).',
      contribution:'The founder of British empiricism and liberal political philosophy. His political theory directly shaped the American Declaration of Independence and Constitution, and French Revolutionary thought. His epistemology influenced Hume and Kant.',
      keyideas:'Tabula rasa: the mind is a blank slate at birth; all knowledge derives from experience (sensation and reflection) — no innate ideas. Primary qualities (shape, motion) exist in objects; secondary qualities (colour, taste) are in us. Political: natural rights (life, liberty, property) exist prior to government. Government is a trust — if it violates these rights, citizens have the right to revolution. Separation of powers. Toleration: the state has no authority over private religious belief.',
      legacy:'Thomas Jefferson directly used Locke\'s language in the Declaration of Independence (replacing Locke\'s "property" with "pursuit of happiness"). His toleration arguments are the foundation of liberal democratic theory. His epistemology generated Hume\'s radical empiricism and ultimately led to Kant\'s synthesis.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    leibniz: { name:'Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz', years:'1646–1716', origin:'Leipzig, Holy Roman Empire', col:'#534AB7', bg:'#EEEDFE',
      wrote:'Monadology (1714), Theodicy (1710), New Essays on Human Understanding, Discourse on Metaphysics. He did not publish a magnum opus — his philosophy is reconstructed from essays and letters.',
      contribution:'The other co-inventor of calculus (simultaneously with Newton, independently — the notation we use today is Leibniz\'s). The greatest rationalist metaphysician. Polymathic: he also contributed to logic (anticipating Boolean logic and computer science), history, law, physics, and linguistics.',
      keyideas:'Monads: the world consists of infinitely many indivisible, immaterial substances (monads), each a "windowless" unit that mirrors the entire universe from its perspective. There is no causal interaction between monads — God established "pre-established harmony." This is the best of all possible worlds (God chose to create the world with the greatest possible ratio of perfection to imperfection). The principle of sufficient reason: everything has a reason why it is so rather than otherwise.',
      legacy:'Voltaire savagely mocked his theodicy in Candide ("all is for the best in the best of all possible worlds") — Leibniz became a target for Enlightenment optimism. His logic anticipates Boolean algebra and computation. The principle of sufficient reason is a live and contested principle in analytic metaphysics. His monadology influenced phenomenology (each monad\'s perspective) and Whitehead\'s process philosophy.',
