      contribution:'Developed one of philosophy\'s most radical and counterintuitive positions: subjective idealism. Material objects do not exist independently of minds that perceive them. This sounds eccentric but is actually a rigorous response to the problems Locke\'s empiricism raised about the external world.',
      keyideas:'Esse est percipi: "to be is to be perceived." Objects are collections of ideas in minds. There is no material substance underlying perceptible qualities. But why do objects persist when no one perceives them? Because God eternally perceives everything — Berkeley\'s idealism is a theistic proof. "Dr Johnson, I refute it thus!" (kicking a stone) — Johnson\'s response misses the point: Berkeley never denied we have real experiences of hard objects, only that there is a mind-independent material substance behind those experiences.',
      legacy:'Hume extended Berkeley\'s empiricism to its sceptical conclusion (even the self is a bundle of perceptions, not a substance). Kant\'s philosophy is partly a response to Berkeley. His idealism is deeply unfashionable in analytic philosophy but resurfaces in philosophy of physics (quantum mechanics raises genuine questions about observer-independence).',
