    hume: { name:'David Hume', years:'1711–1776', origin:'Edinburgh, Scotland', col:'#633806', bg:'#FAEEDA',
      wrote:'A Treatise of Human Nature (1739–40), An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748), An Enquiry Concerning the Principles of Morals (1751), Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (published posthumously 1779).',
      contribution:'The most radical empiricist and most penetrating sceptic in the Western tradition. His Treatise "fell dead-born from the press" — but its ideas gradually transformed modern philosophy. Kant said Hume "woke me from my dogmatic slumber." His critiques of causation, induction, and the self remain live and unsolved.',
      keyideas:'All ideas derive from impressions (sensory experience). Causation: we never perceive necessary connection between events — only constant conjunction. Our belief in causation is a habit of the mind. The problem of induction: no number of observed instances logically entails a general law. The is-ought problem (Hume\'s guillotine). The self is a bundle of perceptions — there is no permanent, unified self. Critique of miracles: testimony for miracles is less probable than the natural laws they allegedly violate.',
      legacy:'Hume\'s problem of induction has never been solved — Karl Popper\'s falsificationism was an attempt. His bundle theory of the self was developed in Buddhist philosophy and anticipates neuroscience (Metzinger\'s "no-self" view). His account of causation raised by Kant into the transcendental idealism project. His religious scepticism made him one of the first major figures to articulate a coherent secular ethics.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
