  var enlightenmentPhilosophers = {
    montesquieu: { name:'Montesquieu', years:'1689–1755', origin:'La Brède, France', col:'#0C447C', bg:'#E6F1FB',
      wrote:'The Persian Letters (1721 — satirical epistolary novel), The Spirit of the Laws (1748) — one of the most influential works in political theory.',
      contribution:'The philosopher who made comparative political sociology possible. He studied forms of government empirically and argued that legal and political institutions must fit the natural and social environment of a people. He identified and named the separation of powers (executive, legislative, judicial) that became the model for constitutional democracies worldwide.',
      keyideas:'Three forms of government: republic (virtue as its principle), monarchy (honour), despotism (fear). Separation of powers: the three branches must be separate to prevent tyranny — liberty exists only when power checks power. Laws must be adapted to climate, geography, religion, commerce, and manners. England\'s constitution is the model for political liberty. Critique of slavery and of religious fanaticism.',
      legacy:'The US Constitution\'s separation of powers is directly traceable to Montesquieu. His empirical, comparative approach influenced modern political science and sociology. Tocqueville\'s Democracy in America is a Montesquieuian work.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    rousseau: { name:'Jean-Jacques Rousseau', years:'1712–1778', origin:'Geneva (worked in France)', col:'#085041', bg:'#E1F5EE',
      wrote:'Discourse on Inequality (1755), The Social Contract (1762), Emile (1762), Confessions (posthumous 1782).',
      contribution:'The most radical and influential Enlightenment critic of the Enlightenment. He questioned whether civilisation, science, and the arts had improved morality. His political philosophy inspired the French Revolution. His educational theory (child-led, natural development) influenced progressive education for 250 years.',
      keyideas:'"Man is born free, but everywhere he is in chains." The general will: the genuine will of the political community as a whole (not merely the will of the majority). Sovereignty belongs inalienably to the people. Property is the origin of inequality and most social ills. Natural goodness: humans are naturally good; society corrupts. Education should protect the child\'s natural development from social corruption (Emile).',
      legacy:'The French Revolution\'s ideology drew on the general will — with troubling consequences: Robespierre justified the Terror through the general will. Kant was profoundly influenced by Rousseau\'s moral egalitarianism. His Confessions pioneered modern autobiography and the Romantic movement. His critique of inequality and property influenced socialist thought.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    adamsmith: { name:'Adam Smith', years:'1723–1790', origin:'Kirkcaldy, Scotland', col:'#27500A', bg:'#EAF3DE',
      wrote:'The Theory of Moral Sentiments (1759), An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations (1776).',
      contribution:'Often called the father of modern economics — though he was primarily a moral philosopher. The Wealth of Nations (1776) is the founding text of classical economics. It is widely misread: Smith did not advocate unlimited self-interest but argued that in well-regulated markets, self-interested behaviour often (not always) produces socially beneficial outcomes.',
      keyideas:'Moral Sentiments: sympathy and the impartial spectator are the foundations of morality — we judge our own actions by imagining how an impartial, well-informed observer would evaluate them. Wealth of Nations: division of labour, market prices, the "invisible hand" (the unintended social benefit of individual exchange), labour theory of value. Markets require institutions, legal frameworks, and public goods that only government can provide — Smith was not a libertarian.',
      legacy:'"The father of capitalism" — though Smith would be appalled at many uses of his name. Laissez-faire economics selectively reads Wealth of Nations and ignores Theory of Moral Sentiments. Contemporary economists (Sen, Sandel) have returned to Smith\'s moral philosophy to recover a richer picture. He is one of the Enlightenment\'s most consequential thinkers.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    bentham: { name:'Jeremy Bentham', years:'1748–1832', origin:'Houndsditch, London, England', col:'#444441', bg:'#F1EFE8',
      wrote:'Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789), A Fragment on Government, Constitutional Code.',
      contribution:'Founder of utilitarianism and a radical legal and social reformer. He advocated universal suffrage, freedom of the press, the abolition of slavery, and decriminalisation of homosexuality — all radical positions in his time. His auto-icon (his preserved skeleton and wax head, dressed in his clothes) sits in a cabinet at UCL, London, as he requested.',
      keyideas:'The principle of utility (greatest happiness principle): the right action is the one that maximises pleasure and minimises pain. The hedonic calculus: pleasures and pains can be quantified and compared by intensity, duration, probability, propinquity, fecundity, purity, and extent. "Each to count for one, and none for more than one." The Panopticon: a prison design in which inmates can never know if they are being watched, making constant surveillance unnecessary — Foucault\'s Discipline and Punish made this central to analysis of modern power.',
      legacy:'Utilitarianism became the dominant framework for public policy and economics (welfare economics, cost-benefit analysis). His Panopticon became one of the most discussed architectural metaphors in social theory. Mill\'s more sophisticated utilitarianism was developed partly to address weaknesses in Bentham\'s crude hedonic calculus.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    kant: { name:'Immanuel Kant', years:'1724–1804', origin:'Königsberg, Prussia', col:'#534AB7', bg:'#EEEDFE',
      wrote:'Critique of Pure Reason (1781/1787), Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785), Critique of Practical Reason (1788), Critique of Judgment (1790).',
      contribution:'Perhaps the most important philosopher since Aristotle. Attempted a Copernican revolution in philosophy: instead of asking how the mind conforms to reality, he asked how reality conforms to the mind. The mind imposes structure (space, time, causality) on experience. He synthesised rationalism and empiricism and transformed both ethics and aesthetics.',
      keyideas:'The synthetic a priori: knowledge that is both informative (synthetic) and knowable without experience (a priori) — e.g., "every event has a cause." Phenomena vs noumena: we know things only as they appear to us (structured by the mind\'s categories), never as they are in themselves. The categorical imperative (see Part B). The sublime and the beautiful in aesthetics. Moral autonomy: rational beings legislate moral law to themselves.',
      legacy:'German Idealism (Fichte, Schelling, Hegel) was a direct response to Kant. Rawls\'s theory of justice is Kantian. Korsgaard\'s constitutivism in ethics is Kantian. The analytic/continental divide partly traces to different readings of Kant. His critique of the traditional proofs for God\'s existence is still decisive for many philosophers.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    hegel: { name:'Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel', years:'1770–1831', origin:'Stuttgart, Württemberg', col:'#0C447C', bg:'#E6F1FB',
      wrote:'Phenomenology of Spirit (1807), Science of Logic (1812–16), Encyclopedia of the Philosophical Sciences (1817), Philosophy of Right (1820).',
      contribution:'The most systematic and ambitious philosopher of the modern era. Attempted to construct an all-encompassing philosophical system in which everything — nature, history, art, religion, philosophy — is understood as the progressive self-realisation of the Absolute (Spirit / Geist). Deeply difficult to read — possibly deliberately so.',
      keyideas:'The dialectic: history progresses through the tension of opposites (thesis → antithesis → synthesis / Aufhebung — "sublation" preserves and transforms both). "The real is rational and the rational is real" — the structure of history is rational. The owl of Minerva: philosophy only understands a historical epoch when it is over. The master-slave dialectic (Phenomenology Ch. IV): a seminal analysis of how self-consciousness requires recognition from another, and how the relationship inverts over time.',
      legacy:'Marx inverted Hegel\'s idealism into dialectical materialism. Kierkegaard reacted against Hegel\'s system by insisting on the irreducibility of subjective, individual existence. The master-slave dialectic influenced Sartre, Fanon, and postcolonial theory. Hegel\'s philosophy of history (history has a goal: freedom) shaped liberal and Marxist historical thought.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    kierkegaard: { name:'Søren Kierkegaard', years:'1813–1855', origin:'Copenhagen, Denmark', col:'#712B13', bg:'#FAECE7',
      wrote:'Either/Or (1843), Fear and Trembling (1843), Philosophical Fragments (1844), Concluding Unscientific Postscript (1846), The Sickness Unto Death (1849). Many under pseudonyms.',
      contribution:'The father of existentialism, though he was a profoundly Christian thinker. He attacked Hegel\'s systematic rationalism from within, insisting that the existing individual cannot be captured in any system. He invented indirect communication — using pseudonyms and irony to force readers to work out their own truths.',
      keyideas:'Three stages of existence: aesthetic (the pursuit of pleasure and immediacy — Don Juan), ethical (duty, social norms — the judge), religious (the leap of faith — Abraham). Truth is subjectivity: the most important truths cannot be objectively demonstrated but must be personally appropriated. The leap of faith: belief in God cannot be rationally justified; it requires a paradoxical leap beyond reason. Anxiety (Angst): the dizzying awareness of freedom; the "dizziness of freedom." Despair: the failure to become one\'s true self.',
      legacy:'Kierkegaard is the foundational figure for both 20th-century existentialism (Sartre, Heidegger acknowledge him) and Protestant neo-orthodoxy (Barth, Tillich). His pseudonymous method influenced the novel. His concept of anxiety is appropriated by Heidegger and Sartre. He is studied as much in theology as in philosophy.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    marx: { name:'Karl Marx', years:'1818–1883', origin:'Trier, Rhine Province, Prussia', col:'#712B13', bg:'#FAECE7',
      wrote:'Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts (1844, unpublished), The Communist Manifesto (1848, with Engels), Das Kapital Vol. I (1867). Volumes II and III published posthumously by Engels.',
      contribution:'One of the most influential thinkers in human history — for better and worse. Founded historical materialism: the view that history is driven by material forces (technology, property relations, class conflict), not ideas. His analysis of capitalism — alienation, commodity fetishism, surplus value, class struggle — generated both revolutionary movements and major academic traditions.',
      keyideas:'Historical materialism: the base (economic structure — forces and relations of production) determines the superstructure (legal, political, cultural institutions). Alienation: workers are alienated from their labour (they do not own or control its product), from other workers (competition), and from their species-being (social, creative nature). The labour theory of value. Class struggle: "The history of all hitherto existing society is the history of class struggles." Ideology: the ruling ideas of any epoch are the ideas of the ruling class.',
      legacy:'Marxism shaped the 20th century more than any other philosophical tradition — through communist states, labour movements, and academic traditions (Frankfurt School, structuralism, postcolonial theory). His critique of capitalism remains analytically powerful even for non-Marxists. Few would deny that his analysis of commodity fetishism and ideology are illuminating.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    nietzsche: { name:'Friedrich Nietzsche', years:'1844–1900', origin:'Röcken, Kingdom of Prussia', col:'#633806', bg:'#FAEEDA',
      wrote:'Thus Spoke Zarathustra (1883–85), Beyond Good and Evil (1886), On the Genealogy of Morality (1887), The Birth of Tragedy (1872), The Gay Science (1882).',
      contribution:'The most provocative philosopher of the 19th century. Declared the death of God and its consequences for morality and culture. Developed a radical critique of traditional morality, systematic philosophy, and the ascetic ideal. His influence spans philosophy, literature, psychology (Freud, Jung acknowledged him), and politics (grotesquely misappropriated by the Nazis).',
      keyideas:'"God is dead and we have killed him" — not an argument but a cultural diagnosis. The will to power: the fundamental drive of all life is not self-preservation but expansion, creation, overcoming. The Übermensch (Overman): the ideal of a person who creates their own values rather than inheriting them. Eternal recurrence: what if you had to live your life over infinite times? Would you embrace it (amor fati)? The genealogy of morals: "slave morality" (Christianity, utilitarianism) inverts the noble values of strength and affirmation into resentment of strength.',
      legacy:'Nietzsche is one of the most widely read philosophers in the world but also one of the most misread. His sister Elisabeth falsified his manuscripts for Nazi purposes. Existentialism (Heidegger, Sartre, Camus) is largely a response to the death of God problem he posed. Foucault\'s genealogical method is explicitly Nietzschean.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
  };

