  var c20Philosophers = {
    russell: { name:'Bertrand Russell', years:'1872–1970', origin:'Trellech, Wales', col:'#0C447C', bg:'#E6F1FB',
      wrote:'Principia Mathematica (1910–13, with Whitehead), The Problems of Philosophy (1912), History of Western Philosophy (1945), Why I Am Not a Christian (1927).',
      contribution:'Co-founder of analytic philosophy with Frege and Moore. Discovered Russell\'s Paradox (undermining Frege\'s set theory). With Whitehead, attempted to derive all of mathematics from logic alone (logicism) — Gödel\'s incompleteness theorems showed this cannot be completed. Won the Nobel Prize in Literature (1950).',
      keyideas:'Theory of descriptions ("the present King of France is bald" — how can this be false rather than meaningless if there is no referent? The logical form is "there exists an x such that x is a present King of France, and x is bald, and for all y, if y is a present King of France then y=x"). Logical atomism: the world consists of atomic facts; ideal language maps directly onto these. Russell\'s Paradox: the set of all sets that do not contain themselves — does it contain itself? Robust advocacy of empiricism, secularism, and nuclear disarmament.',
      legacy:'Russell\'s theory of descriptions is one of the most celebrated pieces of philosophical analysis. His paradox forced the reconstruction of the foundations of mathematics. His popular History of Western Philosophy introduced millions of readers to philosophy. He cofounded CND and was arrested at 89 for nuclear disarmament protests.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    wittgenstein: { name:'Ludwig Wittgenstein', years:'1889–1951', origin:'Vienna, Austria-Hungary', col:'#534AB7', bg:'#EEEDFE',
      wrote:'Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus (1921 — his only book published in his lifetime), Philosophical Investigations (1953 — posthumous). These two works represent radically different philosophies.',
      contribution:'The only philosopher widely regarded as having created two distinct major philosophical positions. The early Wittgenstein (Tractatus) founded logical atomism. The later Wittgenstein (Investigations) dismantled it and much of the tradition. Arguably the 20th century\'s most influential philosopher on the practice of analytic philosophy.',
      keyideas:'Early: the world is all that is the case — a totality of facts, not things. Language pictures facts. "Whereof one cannot speak, thereof one must be silent" — ethics, aesthetics, and metaphysics are unspeakable. Late: "Meaning is use" — words get their meaning from their role in language games and forms of life, not from picturing facts. The private language argument (see Part N). Family resemblance: concepts often share overlapping similarities without a single common feature (games). Philosophical problems arise from misuses of language; the task is philosophical therapy.',
      legacy:'The Tractatus influenced logical positivism (the Vienna Circle misread it as endorsing their views — Wittgenstein did not). The Investigations shaped Oxford ordinary language philosophy, philosophy of mind, social science methodology, and philosophy of psychology. His PI is the most discussed text in analytic philosophy of the 20th century.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    heidegger: { name:'Martin Heidegger', years:'1889–1976', origin:'Meßkirch, Germany', col:'#27500A', bg:'#EAF3DE',
      wrote:'Being and Time (1927), "The Origin of the Work of Art" (1935), "The Question Concerning Technology" (1954), Introduction to Metaphysics (1953).',
      contribution:'The most important and most controversial philosopher of the continental tradition. His analysis of human existence (Dasein) in Being and Time is the most systematic phenomenological ontology. His thought profoundly influenced existentialism, hermeneutics, deconstruction, and environmental philosophy. His involvement with the Nazi Party (he was rector of Freiburg University under the Nazis in 1933–34 and never unambiguously retracted) remains deeply troubling and debated.',
      keyideas:'Being-in-the-world (In-der-Welt-sein): humans are not subjects facing an objective world — we are always already embedded in a world of purposes, tools, and relationships. Dasein (being-there): the mode of being that questions its own being. Thrownness (Geworfenheit): we are thrown into a world we did not choose — language, history, culture. Being-toward-death: Dasein is the being for which its own death is always a possibility; authentic existence means owning this. They-self (das Man): the inauthentic mode of losing oneself in what "one does," what "they say." The ready-to-hand (Zuhandenheit) vs present-at-hand (Vorhandenheit): a hammer in use vanishes into its function; it becomes conspicuous (present-at-hand) only when it breaks.',
      legacy:'His influence on 20th-century philosophy is unparalleled in the continental tradition. Sartre, Merleau-Ponty, Derrida, Gadamer, and Habermas all engage centrally with Heidegger. His analysis of technology as "enframing" (Gestell) — reducing all beings to resources to be optimised — is now central to philosophy of technology. The question of how to read his philosophy in light of his Nazism is unresolved.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    sartre: { name:'Jean-Paul Sartre', years:'1905–1980', origin:'Paris, France', col:'#712B13', bg:'#FAECE7',
      wrote:'Being and Nothingness (1943), Existentialism is a Humanism (1946), Critique of Dialectical Reason (1960), Nausea (novel, 1938), No Exit (play, 1944).',
      contribution:'The most famous philosopher of the 20th century. Brought existentialism to a global audience. His lecture "Existentialism is a Humanism" sold out repeatedly on delivery. Unlike Heidegger, he was politically engaged — a committed leftist who signed manifestos, visited Castro\'s Cuba, and refused the Nobel Prize (1964) to avoid institutionalisation.',
      keyideas:'Existence precedes essence (see Part N, Q5). Bad faith (mauvaise foi): self-deception about one\'s freedom. Radical freedom and radical responsibility — "condemned to be free." The Other as constitutive of self-consciousness: "Hell is other people" (No Exit) — other consciousnesses threaten to reduce me to an object, to define me from the outside. Being-in-itself (en-soi): inert, thing-like being. Being-for-itself (pour-soi): conscious being — always a step ahead of itself, a negation of mere being. Later work attempted to synthesise existentialism with Marxism.',
      legacy:'Sartre\'s influence on literature, psychotherapy, feminist philosophy (de Beauvoir\'s application of existentialism to the situation of women), and political theory is enormous. His public intellectual model — philosopher as engaged public commentator — shaped academic and journalistic culture. "Bad faith" has entered ordinary language.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    arendt: { name:'Hannah Arendt', years:'1906–1975', origin:'Hannover, Germany (later USA)', col:'#3C3489', bg:'#EEEDFE',
      wrote:'The Origins of Totalitarianism (1951), The Human Condition (1958), Eichmann in Jerusalem (1963), The Life of the Mind (posthumous 1978).',
      contribution:'The most important political philosopher of the 20th century. A Jewish philosopher who fled Nazi Germany for the US. Her analysis of totalitarianism was the first systematic account of Nazism and Stalinism as a new form of political evil. Her concept of the "banality of evil" from her Eichmann reporting transformed how we think about moral responsibility and bureaucratic complicity.',
      keyideas:'The banality of evil: Adolf Eichmann was not a monster but a thoughtless bureaucrat — evil can be perpetrated by ordinary people who simply stop thinking. The vita activa (active life) has three modes: labour (biological reproduction), work (building a durable world of things), and action (political speech and deed in the public realm). Action is the highest human activity — it is through political participation that we disclose who we are. Totalitarianism annihilates plurality and spontaneity — the two conditions of political life. The public/private distinction. Natality (the capacity to begin something new) as the political counterpart to mortality.',
      legacy:'Her concept of the banality of evil is one of the most used and debated in moral philosophy. Her work on totalitarianism remained ahead of its time — it described phenomena whose full implications are still being absorbed. Feminist philosophers have engaged extensively with her distinction between public and private. She is one of the few philosophers whose work is cited equally in philosophy, political theory, and history.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    baudrillard: { name:'Jean Baudrillard', years:'1929–2007', origin:'Reims, France', col:'#444441', bg:'#F1EFE8',
      wrote:'The System of Objects (1968), Consumer Society (1970), Simulacra and Simulation (1981), The Gulf War Did Not Take Place (1991).',
      contribution:'The major theorist of postmodernism, hyperreality, and simulation. He argued that in advanced consumer societies, signs no longer refer to any underlying reality — they refer only to other signs. The map precedes the territory. The Gulf War title was deliberately provocative: not that no war occurred, but that the media spectacle had replaced the event itself.',
      keyideas:'Simulacra: copies without originals. Four stages of the image: (1) reflects a basic reality, (2) masks and perturbs a basic reality, (3) masks the absence of a basic reality, (4) bears no relation to any reality. Hyperreality: Disneyland exists to make us believe the rest of America is real — when in fact "real" America is hyperreal. Seduction vs production: postmodern culture moves from a logic of production (things have use-value and exchange-value) to seduction (signs circulate without referents). The desert of the real.',
      legacy:'The Matrix (1999) drew directly on Simulacra and Simulation — the book appears in the film. His concept of hyperreality is now indispensable for analysing social media, reality television, political spectacle, and advertising. He remains controversial: critics argue his provocations are themselves a form of spectacular intellectual seduction.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
    zizek: { name:'Slavoj Žižek', years:'1949–', origin:'Ljubljana, Slovenia', col:'#085041', bg:'#E1F5EE',
      wrote:'The Sublime Object of Ideology (1989), The Plague of Fantasies (1997), The Parallax View (2006), Less Than Nothing (2012 — 1000 pages on Hegel).',
      contribution:'The most widely read continental philosopher of the early 21st century. Synthesises Lacanian psychoanalysis, Hegelian dialectics, and Marxist political theory to analyse contemporary ideology, culture, and politics. Famous for finding philosophical depth in popular culture (Hollywood films, jokes, toilet design) and for his provocations.',
      keyideas:'Ideology is not false consciousness — it is a structural necessity of symbolic reality. The big Other: the symbolic order (language, social norms, law) that structures social reality, but which exists only insofar as subjects believe in it. Fantasy structures reality rather than escaping it. The "parallax view": the same object viewed from two incompatible perspectives, where both perspectives are necessary and neither is correct. Hegelian parallax: contradictions are not to be synthesised away but held in productive tension.',
      legacy:'His public intellectual presence has done more than any living philosopher to popularise continental theory. His analysis of ideology, enjoyment, and the big Other draws on Lacan in ways that have influenced cultural studies, film theory, and political theory. He remains as controversial as he is widely read — some regard him as a profound thinker, others as a brilliant performer whose arguments are ultimately evasive.',
      render: renderPhilosopher },
  };

