    utilitarianism: { name:'Utilitarianism', period:'18th–19th century', figures:'Bentham, Mill, Singer', col:'#085041', bg:'#E1F5EE',
      core:'The right action is the one that produces the greatest good for the greatest number. Moral worth is determined entirely by consequences. Pleasure and pain are the fundamental values.',
      reacted:'Against deontological ethics: why should rules override better outcomes? Against aristocratic and theological ethics that privileged some interests over others.',
      thinkers:'Bentham: hedonic calculus. Each person counts for one. Mill: pleasures differ in quality — poetry is better than pushpin. Freedom as utility-maximising. Peter Singer: effective altruism; animal suffering counts equally.',
