  var metaethicsPositions = {
    realism: { name:'Moral realism', col:'#27500A', bg:'#EAF3DE',
      render: function(d) {
        return `
          <div style="display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; margin-bottom:10px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
            <div style="font-size:15px; font-weight:500; color:var(--color-text-primary);">Moral realism</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:500; background:${d.bg}; color:${d.col}; border-radius:99px; padding:2px 9px;">Cognitivist · Truth-apt</div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Core claim</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Moral facts exist independently of what any person or culture believes. Moral judgements are true or false, and some are genuinely true.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Key versions</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;"><strong>Non-naturalism</strong> (Moore, Plato): moral properties are irreducible, non-natural features of reality. <strong>Naturalism</strong> (Cornell realism): moral properties are identical with or supervene on natural properties. <strong>Robust realism</strong> (Parfit): reasons are as real as mathematical truths.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Strongest argument for</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Moral progress seems real -- the abolition of slavery was not just a change in preference but a genuine moral discovery. This is hard to explain without something like moral facts.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Hardest challenge</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Mackie's argument from queerness: moral properties would be unlike anything else in the natural world, and we would need a mysterious faculty to detect them. Moral epistemology remains underdeveloped.</div></div>
          </div>`;
      }},
    errortheory: { name:'Error theory', col:'#791F1F', bg:'#FCEBEB',
      render: function(d) {
        return `
          <div style="display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; margin-bottom:10px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
            <div style="font-size:15px; font-weight:500; color:var(--color-text-primary);">Error theory</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:500; background:${d.bg}; color:${d.col}; border-radius:99px; padding:2px 9px;">Cognitivist · All false</div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Core claim</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Moral sentences purport to state objective facts (unlike expressivism) -- but since no moral facts exist, all positive moral claims are false. Moral discourse involves systematic error.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Key figures</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">J.L. Mackie (Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong, 1977). Richard Joyce (The Myth of Morality, 2001). Moral fictionalism (Joyce's later view): we should treat moral discourse as useful fiction, like children's games.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Strongest argument for</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">The argument from queerness + argument from relativity. Moral disagreement across cultures is best explained by different social environments, not different epistemic access to the same moral facts.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Hardest challenge</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">It seems to imply that "the Holocaust was wrong" is false, which most find untenable. Moral fictionalism is a possible escape: we retain moral talk as useful fiction without claiming it is literally true.</div></div>
          </div>`;
      }},
    expressivism: { name:'Expressivism', col:'#3C3489', bg:'#EEEDFE',
      render: function(d) {
        return `
          <div style="display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; margin-bottom:10px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
            <div style="font-size:15px; font-weight:500; color:var(--color-text-primary);">Expressivism</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:500; background:${d.bg}; color:${d.col}; border-radius:99px; padding:2px 9px;">Non-cognitivist · Attitude-expressing</div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Core claim</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Moral sentences are not truth-apt -- they do not assert facts but express attitudes, commitments, or plans. "Torture is wrong" is more like "Boo, torture!" than like "Torture causes pain."</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Key versions</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;"><strong>Emotivism</strong> (Ayer, Stevenson): moral sentences express and evoke emotions. <strong>Prescriptivism</strong> (Hare): they issue universal prescriptions. <strong>Quasi-realism</strong> (Blackburn): we can earn the right to talk as if moral claims are true. <strong>Norm-expressivism</strong> (Gibbard): express acceptance of norms for action and feeling.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Strongest argument for</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Avoids the metaphysical puzzles of realism -- no strange moral properties, no mysterious faculty. Explains the motivational force of moral judgements: if they express attitudes, it is unsurprising that they motivate action.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Hardest challenge</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">The Frege-Geach problem (Peter Geach, 1965): "If torture is wrong, then it is wrong to make others torture." If "torture is wrong" only expresses an attitude, this conditional does not make logical sense. Moral logic seems to require that moral sentences have truth values.</div></div>
          </div>`;
      }},
    constructivism: { name:'Constructivism', col:'#0C447C', bg:'#E6F1FB',
      render: function(d) {
        return `
          <div style="display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; margin-bottom:10px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
            <div style="font-size:15px; font-weight:500; color:var(--color-text-primary);">Constructivism</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:500; background:${d.bg}; color:${d.col}; border-radius:99px; padding:2px 9px;">Cognitivist · Mind-dependent</div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Core claim</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Moral truths are constructed by rational procedures or from the perspective of rational agents. Moral facts are real but mind-dependent -- they depend on what idealised rational agents would agree to, not on a mind-independent moral realm.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Key versions</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;"><strong>Rawls</strong>: principles of justice are those chosen behind the veil of ignorance. <strong>Korsgaard</strong> (The Sources of Normativity): obligations arise from reflective endorsement of one's practical identity -- the commitments that make your life your own. <strong>Scanlon</strong> (What We Owe to Each Other): something is wrong if its performance under the circumstances could not be justified by principles no one could reasonably reject.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Strongest argument for</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Avoids both the metaphysical excess of realism and the nihilistic bite of error theory. Explains why moral claims have normative force for rational beings: because they are what reason demands, not merely what someone felt.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Hardest challenge</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">What makes the constructive procedure authoritative? If someone simply refuses to enter the original position or to reflect on their identity, are they beyond moral criticism? Constructivism seems to presuppose the value of rationality it is supposed to derive.</div></div>
          </div>`;
      }},
    relativism: { name:'Moral relativism', col:'#633806', bg:'#FAEEDA',
      render: function(d) {
        return `
          <div style="display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; margin-bottom:10px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
            <div style="font-size:15px; font-weight:500; color:var(--color-text-primary);">Moral relativism</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:500; background:${d.bg}; color:${d.col}; border-radius:99px; padding:2px 9px;">Descriptive · Normative</div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Two distinct claims</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;"><strong>Descriptive relativism</strong>: moral beliefs vary substantially across cultures (an empirical, uncontroversial observation). <strong>Normative relativism</strong>: moral truth is relative to the individual or culture -- there are no cross-cultural moral truths. The second does not follow from the first: disagreement about facts does not show there are no facts.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">The self-refutation problem</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Normative relativism makes the claim that tolerance is universally required -- that we should not impose our moral standards on others. But this is itself a universal moral claim, which relativism says cannot exist. Relativism as a practical guide self-undermines.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">What relativism rightly captures</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Moral humility: our moral beliefs may be systematically biased by our cultural position and should be held with appropriate uncertainty. History of moral progress: many confident moral positions have been revised. Context-sensitivity: the same action may be appropriate or not depending on social context.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Against strong relativism</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Many apparent cross-cultural moral differences reduce to different beliefs about non-moral facts (about the afterlife, about which actions anger which gods) rather than irreducibly different values. And some moral norms -- prohibitions on gratuitous cruelty, requirements of basic reciprocity -- appear in virtually every documented human culture.</div></div>
          </div>`;
      }},
  };

  buildExplorer('metaethics-btns', 'metaethics-display', metaethicsPositions, 'realism');

