  var appliedEthicsDomains = {
    bioethics: { name:'Bioethics', col:'#085041', bg:'#E1F5EE',
      render: function(d) {
        return `
          <div style="display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; margin-bottom:10px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
            <div style="font-size:15px; font-weight:500; color:var(--color-text-primary);">Bioethics</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:500; background:${d.bg}; color:${d.col}; border-radius:99px; padding:2px 9px;">Medicine · Biology · Life</div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">The four principles (Beauchamp and Childress)</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;"><strong>Autonomy</strong>: respect for the patient's right to make informed decisions. <strong>Beneficence</strong>: act in the patient's best interest. <strong>Non-maleficence</strong>: first, do no harm (<em>primum non nocere</em>). <strong>Justice</strong>: fair distribution of healthcare resources. These four principles often conflict; bioethics is largely the study of how to navigate the conflicts.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Live debates</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;"><strong>Euthanasia and assisted dying</strong>: is a right to die consistent with medical ethics? <strong>Triage</strong>: who gets the ventilator when there are not enough? <strong>Gene editing</strong> (CRISPR): somatic editing for disease vs germline editing vs enhancement. <strong>Organ allocation</strong>: market mechanisms vs medical need vs waiting lists. <strong>Informed consent</strong> with cognitively impaired patients.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Landmark cases</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Karen Ann Quinlan (1976): established the right to refuse life-sustaining treatment. Tuskegee syphilis study (1932-72): systematic exploitation of Black patients -- foundational to the ethics of research involving human subjects. Henrietta Lacks (HeLa cells): tissue taken without consent and used globally for decades. He Jiankui (2018): germline-edited babies -- near-universal condemnation.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">The enhancement debate</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Michael Sandel (The Case Against Perfection): genetic enhancement threatens to undermine our appreciation of giftedness and unconditional love. Nick Bostrom and Julian Savulescu: there is a moral imperative to enhance -- if we can reduce disease and improve cognitive function, failing to do so is analogous to allowing preventable harm. The debate cuts across all the major ethical frameworks.</div></div>
          </div>`;
      }},
    aie: { name:'AI ethics', col:'#3C3489', bg:'#EEEDFE',
      render: function(d) {
        return `
          <div style="display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; margin-bottom:10px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
            <div style="font-size:15px; font-weight:500; color:var(--color-text-primary);">AI ethics</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:500; background:${d.bg}; color:${d.col}; border-radius:99px; padding:2px 9px;">Technology · Alignment · Autonomy</div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">The alignment problem</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">How do we ensure that increasingly powerful AI systems pursue goals that are beneficial to humanity? The problem has two parts: (1) specifying what we want (value alignment) -- human values are complex, contextual, and sometimes inconsistent; (2) ensuring the system pursues specified values rather than proxies (instrumental convergence -- a system optimising for any goal may find that acquiring resources and self-preservation are useful sub-goals).</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Near-term issues</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;"><strong>Algorithmic bias</strong>: systems trained on biased data reproduce and amplify those biases in high-stakes contexts (credit, hiring, criminal justice). <strong>Transparency and explainability</strong>: can we understand why an AI system made a decision? <strong>Surveillance and privacy</strong>: facial recognition and behavioural profiling at scale. <strong>Autonomous weapons</strong>: delegating lethal decisions to machines. <strong>Deepfakes and epistemic harm</strong>: synthetic media that destroys the epistemic infrastructure for trust.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Moral status of AI</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Does a sufficiently sophisticated AI have moral status -- does it deserve moral consideration? This depends on contested views about consciousness, sentience, and what grounds moral status. If functional states that influence processing count as something like suffering, large language models may already warrant some consideration. If phenomenal consciousness is required, the question remains radically open. The hard problem means we have no reliable way to detect consciousness even in systems very different from ourselves.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Existential risk and longtermism</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Nick Bostrom (Superintelligence, 2014), Stuart Russell (Human Compatible): a sufficiently capable AI pursuing a goal even slightly misaligned with human values could pose an existential threat. The philosophical stakes: if longtermism is correct and potential future people count morally, the expected disvalue of human extinction is almost inconceivably large. This makes reducing existential risk a dominant moral priority under almost any reasonable moral framework with uncertainty.</div></div>
          </div>`;
      }},
    envenviron: { name:'Environmental ethics', col:'#27500A', bg:'#EAF3DE',
      render: function(d) {
        return `
          <div style="display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; margin-bottom:10px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
            <div style="font-size:15px; font-weight:500; color:var(--color-text-primary);">Environmental ethics</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:500; background:${d.bg}; color:${d.col}; border-radius:99px; padding:2px 9px;">Nature · Climate · Future</div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Anthropocentric vs non-anthropocentric</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;"><strong>Anthropocentrism</strong>: nature has only instrumental value -- it matters because of what it does for humans. Most environmental policy is implicitly anthropocentric. <strong>Biocentrism</strong> (Paul Taylor): all living things have inherent worth. <strong>Ecocentrism</strong> (Aldo Leopold, deep ecology): ecosystems and species have value independent of individual organisms. "A thing is right when it tends to preserve the integrity, stability, and beauty of the biotic community."</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Climate ethics</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Stephen Gardiner (A Perfect Moral Storm): climate change is a perfect storm of ethical difficulty -- global, intergenerational, and requiring collective action where individual defection is rational. Each generation has incentives to pass costs to the next. Standard consequentialism struggles with the non-identity problem (we cannot harm future people by causing them to exist in a changed world). Justice questions: the countries that contributed least to emissions suffer most. What do wealthy nations owe poorer ones?</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Animal ethics</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Peter Singer (Animal Liberation, 1975): the capacity for suffering, not species membership, is the morally relevant criterion. Factory farming causes suffering on a scale that dwarfs any other institution in human history. Tom Regan (The Case for Animal Rights): animals have rights as "subjects of a life" -- a view grounded in inherent value rather than Singer's welfarism. The philosophical argument against speciesism is one of the most logically powerful in applied ethics, yet adoption of its conclusions remains limited.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Future generations</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Edmund Burke: society is a partnership between the dead, the living, and the unborn. Standard democratic and market institutions discount the future heavily -- they aggregate preferences of existing voters and consumers, not future people. Hans Jonas (The Imperative of Responsibility, 1979): with the power to destroy the natural conditions for human life comes a categorical obligation to preserve them. The precautionary principle: where there is uncertainty about potentially catastrophic outcomes, the burden of proof should be reversed.</div></div>
          </div>`;
      }},
    globalJustice: { name:'Global justice', col:'#0C447C', bg:'#E6F1FB',
      render: function(d) {
        return `
          <div style="display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; margin-bottom:10px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
            <div style="font-size:15px; font-weight:500; color:var(--color-text-primary);">Global justice</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:500; background:${d.bg}; color:${d.col}; border-radius:99px; padding:2px 9px;">Poverty · Rights · Obligation</div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Singer's drowning child argument</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">If you walked past a drowning child and could save her at trivial cost, failing to do so would be seriously wrong. Distance and national borders are morally arbitrary. One billion people living in extreme poverty are in an analogous position. If we can prevent this for trivial personal cost (relative to our wealth), we are required to. The logic is compelling but the conclusion is extremely demanding: most affluent people ought to donate a substantial fraction of their income to effective charities.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Nagel vs Pogge on global justice</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Thomas Nagel: robust justice obligations only arise within a political community bound by coercive institutions. Global poverty is a humanitarian issue requiring aid, not a justice issue requiring structural reform. Thomas Pogge (World Poverty and Human Rights): affluent nations actively harm the global poor through the international order they impose and uphold -- international property rights, borrowing privileges for corrupt governments, trade rules that favour rich countries. This is not merely a failure to aid but an active injustice.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Open borders</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Joseph Carens (The Ethics of Immigration): citizens of affluent nations won a birth lottery -- being born in Germany rather than Niger is the modern equivalent of medieval serfdom. Immigration restrictions perpetuate a morally arbitrary global lottery. Michael Walzer: nations are legitimate "communities of character" with rights to self-determination, including control of membership. The debate structures global justice philosophy: is nationality morally like race (morally arbitrary) or like citizenship (legitimately differential)?</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Reparations</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Do present-day states or individuals owe reparations for historical injustices (slavery, colonialism)? Jeremy Waldron's supersession thesis: historical injustices may lose their moral urgency when the world has changed so much that undoing them is no longer coherent or justice-enhancing. Ta-Nehisi Coates and others: current wealth inequalities are direct causal products of historical injustice -- reparations address ongoing effects, not just historical wrongs. The non-identity problem complicates the question: no current person was harmed by slavery (they would not have existed otherwise).</div></div>
          </div>`;
      }},
    sexgender: { name:'Sex, gender & justice', col:'#712B13', bg:'#FAECE7',
      render: function(d) {
        return `
          <div style="display:flex; align-items:baseline; gap:10px; margin-bottom:10px; flex-wrap:wrap;">
            <div style="font-size:15px; font-weight:500; color:var(--color-text-primary);">Sex, gender and justice</div>
            <div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:500; background:${d.bg}; color:${d.col}; border-radius:99px; padding:2px 9px;">Feminist philosophy · Identity</div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Beauvoir's founding insight</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Simone de Beauvoir (The Second Sex, 1949): "One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman." Woman is not a biological given but a social construction -- the product of processes by which a female human being is shaped to occupy a subordinate social role. This is the application of Sartrean existentialism to the situation of women: existence precedes essence, but women are denied the freedom to define their own essence. It inaugurated second-wave feminist philosophy.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Sex vs gender distinction</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Influential 20th-century distinction: <strong>sex</strong> = biological (male/female/intersex); <strong>gender</strong> = social/cultural (man/woman/non-binary, etc.). Judith Butler (Gender Trouble, 1990) pushed further: sex itself is partly a social construction -- biological categories are not neutral descriptions but are produced by the same power relations that produce gender. Gender is performed, not expressed from a pre-social core. Contemporary debates about trans rights engage directly with where the sex/gender distinction should be drawn and what grounds gender identity.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Intersectionality</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;">Kimberle Crenshaw (1989): legal categories like "sex discrimination" and "race discrimination" fail Black women because their experience is not captured by either category alone. General Motors hired Black people (in factories) and women (in clerical jobs) but not Black women. Intersectionality is a methodological framework: social identities interact multiplicatively, not additively. It has since been extended beyond legal analysis to feminist theory, sociology, and political philosophy.</div></div>
            <div class="sub-card"><div class="muted-text-small u-mb-xs">Liberal vs radical feminist ethics</div><div style="font-size:12px; color:var(--color-text-primary); line-height:1.5;"><strong>Liberal feminism</strong> (Wollstonecraft, Mill): women deserve equal rights and opportunities within existing liberal institutions; discrimination is a violation of the core liberal principle of treating individuals as individuals. <strong>Radical feminism</strong>: existing liberal institutions are structured around male dominance -- equal rights within a patriarchal system is insufficient; the system itself must be transformed. <strong>Socialist feminism</strong>: gender oppression cannot be separated from class oppression; both require analysis together.</div></div>
          </div>`;
      }},
  };

  buildExplorer('applied-btns', 'applied-display', appliedEthicsDomains, 'aie');

  /* ──────────────────────────────────────────
     PART M: MORAL REASONING SELF-CHECK
  ────────────────────────────────────────── */
  var moralCheckItems = [
    { id:'mc1', left:'Moral intuitions should guide our reasoning', right:'Moral intuitions are unreliable and should be overridden by theory' },
    { id:'mc2', left:'Rules matter regardless of outcomes', right:'Outcomes are all that ultimately matter' },
    { id:'mc3', left:'Our obligations to family and friends are stronger than obligations to strangers', right:'All persons deserve equal moral consideration regardless of relationship' },
    { id:'mc4', left:'Moral norms are universal across cultures', right:'Moral norms are culturally specific' },
    { id:'mc5', left:'Individual rights constrain what can be done even for the greater good', right:'The greater good can always override individual rights' },
  ];

  var mcContainer = document.getElementById('moralcheck-items');

  moralCheckItems.forEach(function(item) {
    var row = document.createElement('div');
    row.style.cssText = 'margin-bottom:14px;';
    row.innerHTML = `
      <div style="display:flex; justify-content:space-between; font-size:11px; color:var(--color-text-secondary); margin-bottom:3px; gap:8px;">
        <span style="max-width:45%;">${item.left}</span>
        <span style="max-width:45%; text-align:right;">${item.right}</span>
      </div>
      <input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" class="ethicsslider" id="${item.id}" style="margin-bottom:2px;">`;
    mcContainer.appendChild(row);
  });

  function updateMoralCheck() {
    var vals = moralCheckItems.map(function(item) {
      return parseInt(document.getElementById(item.id).value);
    });
    var avg = vals.reduce(function(a,b){ return a+b; }, 0) / vals.length;
    var intuition = 100 - vals[0];
    var deonto = 100 - vals[1];
    var partial = 100 - vals[2];
    var universal = 100 - vals[3];
    var rights = 100 - vals[4];

    var profile = [];
    if (intuition > 65) profile.push('strong weight on moral intuitions (intuitionsim / Ross)');
    if (intuition < 35) profile.push('preference for overriding intuitions with theory (rationalism / Singer)');
    if (deonto > 65) profile.push('deontological leanings (rules matter intrinsically)');
    if (deonto < 35) profile.push('consequentialist leanings (outcomes are primary)');
    if (partial > 65) profile.push('agent-partiality: special obligations to those close to us');
    if (partial < 35) profile.push('impartiality: equal consideration for all');
    if (universal > 65) profile.push('moral universalism');
    if (universal < 35) profile.push('moral relativism or contextualism');
    if (rights > 65) profile.push('rights-based constraints on maximisation');
    if (rights < 35) profile.push('aggregate welfare can override individual rights');

    var result = document.getElementById('moralcheck-result');
    if (profile.length === 0) {
      result.innerHTML = 'Your responses are balanced across all five dimensions -- a centrist moral epistemology. Most professional ethicists hold nuanced, mixed positions.';
    } else {
      result.innerHTML = '<strong>Your reasoning profile:</strong> ' + profile.join('; ') + '. Note: this is a rough sketch, not a diagnosis -- real moral reasoning combines elements from multiple traditions.';
    }
  }

  moralCheckItems.forEach(function(item) {
    document.getElementById(item.id).addEventListener('input', updateMoralCheck);
  });
  updateMoralCheck();

  
</script>
